    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im studying for my security+ exam and im a bit stuck on password storage. if we already use a hashing algorithm like SHA-256 to scramble the password, why do we actually need to add a \"salt\" to it? doesnt the hash already make it unreadable?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hashing algorithms like SHA-256 are indeed designed to scramble the password, making it unreadable. However, the issue with using a single hash value is that it's like trying to find a specific grain of sand on a beach - even if you have the exact same password, the hash values will be different due to the inherent randomness of the hashing process. This is where salts come in, like a map to help you navigate the beach.\n\nThink of a salt as a unique identifier that's added to the password before hashing. This way, even if two users have the same password, their hash values will be different due to the different salts used. It's like having a personalized key to unlock the password, making it much harder for an attacker to use precomputed tables (rainbow tables) to crack the password.\n\nFor example, imagine two users, Alice and Bob, both with the password \"password123\". Without a salt, their hash values might be the same, making it easier for an attacker to crack the password. But with a salt, Alice's hash value might be \"password123$abc123\" and Bob's hash value might be \"password123$def456\". Even if an attacker finds a precomputed table for \"",
      "source": "target",
