    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i get the part about the hashes being different now. so the salt basically just breaks the pattern of people having the same passwords. but how does it specifically stop the rainbow table thing you mentioned? like, how does adding that extra bit of random stuff make it so a precomputed table won't work?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're really digging into the nitty-gritty of password storage now. The salt helps prevent rainbow table attacks by making it so that even if an attacker has a precomputed table of hashes for common passwords, they won't match the hash of your password.\n\nThink of it like a treasure map. If you have a map with a specific location marked, an attacker can use that map to find the treasure. But if you add a random X to the map, like a tiny tattoo on your ankle, the attacker won't be able to use their map to find the treasure. The X is like the salt - it's a unique identifier that makes the map useless.\n\nIn the case of rainbow tables, an attacker has a map (the precomputed table) that shows the hashes for common passwords. But when they try to use the map to find the hash for your password, the salt is like the X on your ankle - it's a random identifier that doesn't match anything on the map. So even if the attacker has a precomputed table, they won't be able to find a match for your password.\n\nIt's worth noting that the salt itself doesn't need to be stored securely - it can be stored in plain text, like a",
      "source": "target",
